========================================================================================================
[8] DIRECTION: the inequality is a LOWER bound; P needs an UPPER bound
========================================================================================================
  exact rewrite:   C(2t) = (1 + K(t)) C(t)^2 / C(0),   K(t) = Var_nu(e^{-st})/C(t)^2.
  * the inequality is exactly K >= 0 -- it says a floor at t PROPAGATES to 2t, i.e.
    it CERTIFIES a platform and can never exclude one.
  * P wants C_L(L) <= r0 e^{-sqrt(c)}: an upper bound at the LONGEST time.
  * the upper-bound half of Cauchy-Schwarz reads C(t) <= sqrt(C(0)C(2t)): small at 2t
    implies small at t -- backwards.  To reach t = L from t < L one needs the reverse
    inequality  C(2t) <= C(t)^2/C(0)  (log-concavity), which is FALSE in general.
  * the only usable weakening is  K(t) <= K0  uniformly, giving
        C(2t) <= (1+K0) C(t)^2/C(0).
    HZ1 sec.2.2's propagation rho -> rho^2/M0 is exactly K0 = 0, which is assumed,
    not proved (and is false for every model except a single atom).

  HZ1 sec.5.3 [10] reproduced (rho0=0.18, M0=1):
    0.18  ->  0.0324  ->  0.00105  ->  1.102e-06  ->  1.214e-12
    = 0.18 / 3.24e-2 / 1.05e-3 / 1.10e-6 / 1.21e-12, matching HZ1 exactly  [T]
    but every entry is a LOWER bound on C(2^k L): the chain is a platform certificate,
    not a falsifier.  From a floor it produces weaker floors, never an upper bound.

